  "question": {
    "text": "An <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AC</mi></math> circuit drawing power from a source of angular frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;has a power factor of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>0.6</mtext></math>.&nbsp;In this condition, a resistance of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>&nbsp;is present and the current is lagging behind the voltage. If a capacitor is connected in series, then the required capacitance that will result in a power factor of unity is",
    "image": null
  },
